WASHINGTON (Reuters) - The United States on Thursday shut down cotton imports from 26 Chinese companies as part of its efforts to eliminate forced labor on the Uighurs. US supply chain.
Reuters reports that these companies are the latest to be included in the list of import bans on the Ugyhur Compulsory Labor Protection Act, which restricts imports of related goods. To what the US government has identified as an ongoing genocide of ethnic minorities in China's Xinjiang region.
The US Department of Homeland Security said in a statement that the cotton companies are based outside of Xinjiang, but their cotton sources are from Xinjiang.
The Department of Homeland Security wrote: "Putting them on the banned list will increase transparency and ensure that responsible companies can scrutinize their supply chains to ensure they do not include goods. "Produced by forced labor."
According to Reuters, US officials believe that Chinese authorities have set up work camps for Uighurs and other Muslim minorities in western China's Xinjiang region. But Beijing has denied any wrongdoing.
According to the US Department of Homeland Security, Washington has restricted imports from 65 companies since the Uighurs law was enacted in 2021.
