International Analysis: What does Putin want in China?
Xi greeted Putin on the red carpet outside the Great Hall of the People in Beijing, where they were greeted by the Chinese People's Liberation Army (PLA). Waving Chinese and Russian flags.
China and Russia declared a "borderless" partnership in February 2022, when Putin visited Beijing just days before sending tens of thousands of troops into Ukraine, sparking a major land war. The worst in Europe since World War II.
Xi, 70, and Putin, 71, signed a joint statement on Thursday calling for a "new era" against the United States on security issues and a common vision on everything. From Taiwan and Ukraine to North Korea and nuclear weapons, including nuclear technology cooperation, new peace and finance.
"Sino-Russian relations today are difficult to find and both sides must love and nurture this relationship," Xi told Putin. He stressed China's willingness to work together to achieve the development and rehabilitation of their respective countries and promote universal justice and fairness.
Putin and Xi also shared a broad view of the West, which sees the West as gloomy and backward as China faces US dominance in intelligence technology and military force.
Xi and Putin believed that the post-Cold War era of American supremacy was collapsing after the humiliation left by the collapse of the Soviet Union in 1991 and centuries of European colonial rule over China.
