The International Energy Agency (IEA) on Wednesday cut its forecast for 2024 oil demand growth, leading to further differences between the agency and the Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC). Concerning the global oil demand outlook this year. According to Reuters.
The Paris-based energy watchdog has lowered its energy growth forecast for this year to 140,000 barrels per day (bpd) to 1.1 million barrels per day, mostly due to the downturn. Demand for oil in developed countries that are members of the OECD.
The vast differences between the two forecasts are in part due to differing views on the speed of the world's transition to clean fuels.
According to Reuters, the IEA said in its monthly oil report that the drop in its 2024 oil demand forecast was due to poor industrial activity and low winter oil consumption, especially in Europe. Dropping diesel cars is already reducing energy consumption.
"Combined with declining US diesel deliveries earlier this year, it was enough to push oil demand in OECD member states in the first quarter back to a slump," the IEA said. The Thai Ambassador to Cambodia reaffirms position to expand health cooperation with Cambodia
