(Phnom Penh): The National Library of Cambodia was established on December 24, 1924 by the French colonial government in Indochina. In 1954, after gaining independence from the French colony, the library was renamed from the Central Library to the National Library of Cambodia. During the Pol Pot regime from 1975-1979, most of the library staff were brutally killed and the library was severely damaged.
The National Library of Cambodia, as well as most of the buildings in Phnom Penh, was revived after a regime ruled by the Khmer Rouge. Some of the document resources remain to this day. The library continues to store valuable manuscripts and book resources from the early years of the French colonial era.
The National Library reopened in 1980 under the auspices of the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts. Since then, the National Library has struggled to reorganize its services with almost unskilled staff and insufficient funds to organize and collect library books.
The UNESCO National Commission has taken care of old documents, providing projects for the preservation and protection of materials, repairing books and publishing national inventories. The Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) and ASEAN Plus Three provided scanners for scanning old documents about Cambodia as electronic documents and organized training courses for National Library officials.
The ceremony was presided over by Dr. Eang Sophalleth, Minister of Environment, along with several leaders of the Ministry of Environment this morning, May 15, 2024 at the Ministry of Environment.
Dr. Eang Sophalleth emphasized that the campaign "Cambodia is clean, Khmer can do it" is a campaign that clearly shows the commitment of Cambodians across the country to clean up the environment around their homes after Cambodia participated in preventing and preventing the use and disposal of plastic bags. More than just throwing it in public has already been successful.
"The success of this new campaign goes hand in hand with the campaign , which was launched on September 1, 2023, which until now has With the participation of more than 8 million students and people across Cambodia. This is the additional emphasis of the Minister of Environment.
