(USA): A young Cambodian woman, Miss On Monita, was given the opportunity to speak at a meeting of the United Nations on the topic of "war, ending conflicts and the role of women peacekeepers in the world" and highlighted the country's image. Cambodia and nation building at this summit as well.
Ms. On Monita, a Cambodian student who graduated from a prestigious university in Switzerland and received many degrees and diplomas through her ability, has participated in major international forums and conferences in the past. One of the prides of Cambodia, having a Cambodian child, shows the world government the progress and leadership of the state under the shadow of peace to world leaders.
Once again, On Monita was given the opportunity to attend the framework of a UN meeting earlier this week. In front of world leaders and relevant foreign officials who attended the summit, this beautiful young Cambodian woman highlighted the role of women in peace building.
Ms. Monita said that the absence of women from peace talks undermines their sustainability, including women, not only about equality, but also brings about valuable vision and strategy. Nation 1325 on "Women of Peace and Security" marks an important step towards recognizing the role of women.
She highlighted the Cambodian women who endured so much during the Khmer Rouge era for almost four years, and that Cambodian women are very important in advocating for women's participation in peace building. Cambodian women have played a key role in promoting economic development, community healing and advocating for justice.
"Women's participation in peacebuilding helps prevent future conflicts by promoting a culture of peace and understanding," she said. "Cambodia ranks first among ASEAN countries and 13th out of 120 countries in sending women soldiers to UN peacekeeping operations."
At the same time, Ms. Monita also highlighted the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) and women. At this point, she stated that the 17 United Nations Sustainable Development Goals provide a framework to address global issues such as poverty, inequality and climate change.
As for empowering young women, she shared that cultural norms often limit young women to local roles by limiting their potential. We must break away from these patterns and empower them. Young women have unique perspectives, experiences and potential to drive positive change. Their voices are important for policies that integrate social cohesion and address the root causes of conflict.
"By encouraging young women to participate in peacebuilding, we have opened up the creativity and resilience needed for a peaceful and prosperous Cambodia," she said.
It should be noted that on March 28, 2024, Ms. On Monita, a potential young leader from Cambodia, was also appointed as the Youth Ambassador for Peace and attended the 55th Session of the Council Meeting. Human rights at the United Nations as well, showing great pride in representing the country on such an important forum.
