However, farmers and exporters are worried that this year's pepper exports could be in trouble due to the hot weather, which has reduced pepper yields by 60%.
China allowed Cambodia to export pepper directly to the Chinese market in May 2023 after Cambodia completed all procedures. Twenty-eight pepper plantations and seven packaging plants are registered and licensed by the Chinese side for export.
She said that after the promotion, some Chinese guests contacted the company to visit and partner to export more.
However, she worries that the hot weather this year will significantly reduce pepper yields, which will also affect exports.
She said farmers' pepper harvests were less productive due to the hot weather, which killed pepper plants. In fact, every year per hectare farmers can harvest about 3 tons, but this year only half a ton or less.
"It's a lot different in terms of collection compared to previous years," she added. "However, we will try to work with farmers to meet market demand."
Mr. Nguon Lay, President of Kampot Pepper Promotion Association, expressed his pleasure that Cambodian pepper has been expanded to China, a large market that makes ordinary pepper and Kampot pepper better market to help farmers. But while the pepper market is better this year, farmers are having problems because the hot weather has affected pepper yields.
He said that the decline in pepper production this year will make Cambodia unable to supply enough export markets, while every year Kampot pepper exported more than 100 tons, but this year harvest can only be 50 tons. .
