Deputy Prime Minister Hang Chuon Naron announces the introduction of Chinese calligraphy in the new generation school program of Preah Yukanthor High School
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Hang Chuon Naron, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Education, Youth and Sports, announced the introduction of Chinese calligraphy in the new generation school program of Preah Yukanthor High School to expand understanding and exchange culture, philosophy and art between the Cambodian people and the people. Chinese.
The announcement of the introduction of Chinese calligraphy in the new generation school program at Preah Yukunthor High School took place on Saturday, May 11, 2024 at Preah Yukunthor High School.
Tong Vannak, president of the Cambodian Secondary Students' Education and Orientation Association, said Chinese literature was developed in a profound way and reflected the wisdom and philosophy of education through handwriting on white paper with a brush. Dipped in Chinese ink for thousands of years.
The President of the Association continued that the establishment of this Chinese calligraphy class is very important for Cambodian students, not only to speak Chinese, but also to be able to write Chinese calligraphy, especially to learn. The meaning of the content according to the educational philosophy embedded in Chinese literature and calligraphy is clear.
Ly Huot, president of the Khmer-Chinese Calligraphy Association, said Chinese calligraphy has a history of about 5,000 years. Mr. Ly Huot expressed his pleasure in cooperating in teaching Chinese calligraphy to Cambodian students to contribute to strengthening the friendship between the people of the two countries, especially in the field of calligraphy, culture and art.
On that occasion, Deputy Prime Minister Hang Chuon Naron expressed his support for the Chinese calligraphy class in the new generation school program to expand understanding and exchange culture, philosophy and art between the Cambodian and Chinese people.
The Deputy Prime Minister also said that learning Chinese calligraphy, students will be educated along with Chinese philosophies such as the philosophy of Confucian scholars, Lao scholars, etc., which all help to cultivate good ideas for them. In addition, it also provides additional skills for Cambodian students to be imaginative, creative, long-term thinkers, especially to help attract students interested in learning Chinese.
He added that Cambodia and China have the oldest culture in the region and the world and have been in contact with each other since the early Funan period to the present. In 2024, this is the year of people-to-people exchanges between Cambodia and China, so the arrangement of Chinese calligraphy classes will also promote further exchanges between the people of the two countries.
Deputy Prime Minister Hang Chuon Naron also encouraged the continuation of the mission to promote Chinese language education more widely and also encouraged the Chinese calligraphy competition to provide opportunities for talented students to participate in expressing their talents. Body.
