(Phnom Penh): In the past, rice traders often mentioned the problem of lack of capital and difficulty in obtaining credit, which made them unable to afford to buy rice for storage and processing into rice for export. Exit.
To facilitate access to credit for rice entrepreneurs, the Credit Guarantee Corporation of Cambodia (CGCC) has launched the Rice Export Credit Scheme (REGS) to assist rice mill owners. Able to expand its scope to collect rice stocks for milling, processing, exporting, creating and increasing value-added products locally.
Mr. Ros Silva, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, said that providing credit guarantees for the rice sector is very important because the rice sector is a potential sector of Cambodia that receives attention from the Royal Government. And the launch of this project is paving the way for businesses in the sector to have easy access to finance.
He said that due to the risks in the rice sector, some commercial banks did not dare to decide to provide credit to rice traders. However, through projects in collaboration with seven financial institutions, solutions will be found.
According to the Secretary of State for rice processing companies, exporters and millers can afford a maximum loan of up to $ 3 million, with the CGCC providing a guarantee of 80% of the total loan amount.
He emphasized that the maximum loan size of $ 3 million is suitable for processing companies, exporters and mill owners to expand their business scope. He hopes that this work will contribute to achieving the goal of the Royal Government's policy of exporting 1 million tons of rice.
He encouraged the speedy release of the $ 30 million and encouraged rice exporters and millers to use the credit efficiently and responsibly and to use it as a working capital in the short term. To procure rice for export rice production to benefit rice exports.
Ms. Seng Leap, owner of Sek Meas rice mill in Kampong Cham province, which has been doing business in this sector for about 30 years, told Fresh News that the credit guarantee scheme for the rice sector is paving the way for mills and exporters. Rice to easily get more capital to buy rice during the harvest season, which allows her as well as other mill owners to expand the purchase of rice for storage.
She said that in the past, getting a loan from a bank did not seem easy and all required collateral, but through this project, only study the actual business plan, which is easier for those who have a clear business plan.
As a company that exports both rice and buys rice for local supply, she believes that through this credit guarantee scheme, it will help to expand her business.
