(Phnom Penh): Mr. Sin Chanserivutha, Undersecretary of State and Spokesperson of Civil Aviation, confirmed that in the next 3 years, in 2027, Cambodia's air transport sector may recover 100%. To 2019.
Mr. Sin Chanserivutha stated, "Cambodia has previously predicted and concluded that the 100% recovery after Kovid will be in 2024 or early 2025, but after review due to various crises. "Recurrences in the world are not as expected, according to a new forecast by the International Transport Association, Cambodia's growth will recover to 2019 in 2027."
Mr. Sin Chanserivutha said that today, the air transport sector in Cambodia has a recovery of about 63 percent in 2024, so it is not until 2027 that the air transport sector in Cambodia recovers 100 percent. Equivalent to 2019.
Please be informed that in 2019, the number of passengers passing through Cambodia International Airport was 11,634,953. While Phnom Penh International Airport handled 6.02 million passengers, Siem Reap International Airport handled 3.92 million passengers.
In terms of freight, Phnom Penh International Airport received 77,181 tons in 2019, and this size has increased rapidly over the past decade. Senior Civil Aviation official: Cambodia's two largest international airports will promote better air freight (Video inside)
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Sin Chansery Vutha, Undersecretary of State and Spokesman for the Secretariat of State for Civil Aviation of Cambodia, emphasized that at present, Cambodia's air cargo is still limited due to the lack of capacity of Cambodia's airports. To receive large cargo planes.
However, the new Siem Reap Angkor International Airport, which opened last year and the Techo International Airport, which is set to open next year, will serve to improve air freight.
He noted that air freight through Cambodia's three airports, Phnom Penh International Airport, Sihanoukville International Airport and Siem Reap International Airport, saw that the largest air freight was in Phnom Penh next. To Sihanoukville and Siem Reap, there is little freight.
He said that air cargo was still limited due to the fact that Cambodia does not yet have large cargo planes landing due to a number of factors, especially infrastructure, namely that the airport is not qualified to receive. It is not the large aircraft that push some companies to carry only small and medium aircraft.
However, he said that the new Siem Reap Angkor Airport has enough equipment and materials to facilitate transportation, ready to receive large cargo aircraft. At the same time, Techo International Airport, which is ready to open next year, has built the infrastructure to receive large-scale cargo aircraft.
"We think that after the opening of the Techo International Airport in 2025, we expect to transport about 10,000 tons of cargo a year," he added. The capacity of the warehouse at Techo International Airport is sufficient to facilitate the entry and exit of large aircraft.
