US sends destroyer across Taiwan Strait as new Taiwanese leader prepares to be sworn in
(Taipei) - A US destroyer crossed the Taiwan Strait on Wednesday, May 8, less than two weeks later, Taiwan's new leader will be officially sworn in, urging condemnation. Both outraged by Beijing. This is according to a release by Reuters.
China regards Taiwan as one of its provinces and has control over the Taiwan Strait. But the United States and Taiwan claim the strait as international waters. The U.S. Seventh Fleet said the destroyer armed with the USS Halsey guided normal routine transit operations Wednesday through the waters, where freedom of navigation and flight were exercised in parallel. According to international law.
However, the Chinese military considers the passage of the Taiwan Strait by US destroyers to be a distraction to the public and that it has sent warships and aircraft to monitor the activities of US warships.
