Indonesia's economy grows faster than expected in first quarter
JAKARTA: Indonesia's economy grew faster than expected in the first quarter of this year, driven by higher public spending for the general election, but maintaining a strong pace will be difficult due to global developments and Tense local monetary situation.
The economy of Southeast Asia's largest economy grew 5.11 percent from the same period last year, the highest in three quarters, Reuters reported. That was higher than the 5 percent estimate in a Reuters poll and 5.04 percent in the fourth quarter of last year.
In the first quarter of this year, higher election campaign spending on February 14 and higher household spending during Ramadan, which began in March, boosted economic activity.
This has helped offset the slowdown in the economy, which has suffered from declining exports in the past year. Indonesia, a resource-rich country, is the world's largest exporter of coal for heating, palm oil and nickel, among other goods.
According to Reuters, some economists say the increase in election spending and Ramadan spending is an occasional factor. In the coming days, US long-term high interest rates, tensions in the Middle East, as well as Indonesia's direct rate hike cycle totaled 275 basis points (2.75%) from mid-2022 to April. 2024 is hurting the growth outlook.
The economy of Southeast Asia's largest economy grew 5.11 percent from a year earlier, the highest growth rate in three quarters, Reuters reported. That was higher than the 5 percent estimate in a Reuters poll and 5.04 percent in the fourth quarter of last year.
According to Reuters, some economists say the increase in election spending and Ramadan spending is an occasional factor. In the coming days, US long-term high interest rates, tensions in the Middle East, as well as Indonesia's direct rate hike cycle totaled 275 basis points (2.75%) from mid-2022 to April. 2024 is affecting the growth outlook.
