General Knowledge: What is the most important black box for plane crash investigation?
Black boxes are generally divided into two types, one for recording various aircraft data during travel, and it is called FDR. Another type of recording in the cockpit, plus a conversation between pilots, called CVR.
The FDR black box records data such as speed, altitude, acceleration and fuel flow of the aircraft, and it can store this data for the last 25 hours. The CVR black box records all conversations between the pilots as well as the conversations between the pilots and the air traffic controllers. The sound of the switch and the engine are also recorded by the CVR, while its sound storage capacity is limited to the last 2 hours.
