Tensions have risen in Rakhine state since the Arakan Army (AA) launched an attack on security forces in November, ending a ceasefire that has been in place since the military coup in 2021.
Although the Myanmar military continues to maintain control of Sittwe, the state capital, Arakanese (AA) fighters have taken control of the surrounding area, including bases near the borders with India and Bangladesh.
The Arakan Army (AA) is one of several ethnic armed groups in Myanmar's border areas involved in a protracted conflict with the military over autonomy and violence. Managing strategic resources since the country gained independence from the United Kingdom in 1948. The group claims to be advocating for greater autonomy for the Rakhine minority.
Clashes between the Arakan Army (AA) and the military in 2019 resulted in the displacement of about 200,000 people in the region. Rakhine State has also been devastated by violence against the Rohingya minority, with the military crackdown in 2017 causing widespread displacement and prompting international condemnation and UN genocide cases. National.
